<p class="lt-chem-194555"><u>Exercise 14.3.1:</u></p>

<p class="lt-chem-194555">A Lewis acid atom attracts electrons from a Lewis base.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-194555">The most common feature of a Lewis acid is an atom that is not &quot;electronically saturated&quot; or has not filled its octet. For example, an aluminum with only six electrons rather than eight is Lewis acidic.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-194555">Other atoms, like transition metals, have &quot;octets&quot; of eighteen electrons, so having fewer than eighteen electrons in their valence shell can make these atoms Lewis acidic.</p>

